WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:10.330
Earthquakes.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:14.690
Earthquakes are one of the natural phenomena
that have impacted the history of mankind

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:18.200
the most.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:29.939
They have destroyed entire cities and taken
the lives of millions and millions of people.

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:31.390
That’s nothing new.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:36.440
What is new, is knowing how to resist them,
how to survive and how to make safer buildings

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:37.440
and infrastructure.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.330
That, folks, is a very new phenomenon.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:43.500
And when it comes to earthquake proofing,
there are two countries that have had to learn

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.500
a lot.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:55.650
Folks, Chile and Japan are the two most seismically
active countries on the planet.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:00.420
Together they account for around 40% of all
seismic activity that happens in the world.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:05.610
In fact, Chile has suffered the most powerful
earthquake ever recorded: the Valdivia earthquake

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:06.780
in 1960.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.939
It recorded a magnitude of 9.5 on the Richter
scale.

00:01:09.939 --> 00:01:11.820
A true cataclysm.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:18.650
But this is not so unusual when you consider
that both Japan and Chile lie directly above

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:23.710
the Ring of Fire, a stretch of 40,000 kilometers
that accounts for the most seismic and volcanic

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:24.830
activity on earth.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:30.080
In this zone, tectonic plates subduct, that
is, one slides under the other, generating

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:31.570
subduction zones.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:36.150
The movement of the plates is not constant
or regular, it happens with sudden pulls,

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:40.910
and when both plates are very stressed and
have a lot of accumulated energy, they yield,

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:44.550
let go and move suddenly, generating strong
earthquakes.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:49.131
That’s why 90% of all earthquakes in the
world and 80% of the most powerful ones, the

00:01:49.131 --> 00:01:51.440
megathrust, happen in the Ring of Fire.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.660
In fact, Japan and Chile’s earthquakes are
very similar in magnitude and duration.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:03.760
As you can imagine, this challenge comes with
one choice: Adapt or die.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.119
Today, on VisualPolitik, we’ll tell you
how these two countries managed to adapt,

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:12.730
what the main differences between them are
and the great common challenge that Japan

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.950
and Chile face.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.930
Listen up.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:26.360
(CHILE AND JAPAN, LEARNING FROM MISTAKES)

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:30.180
Folks, the most destructive earthquakes have
terrible consequences.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.590
We’re talking about phenomena that can end
the lives of tens of thousands of people in

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:39.700
just a few minutes.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:45.510
Chile and Japan are the most advanced countries
in seismic disaster prevention for one simple

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.060
reason, throughout their history they’ve
undergone terrible tragedies that have taught

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:50.870
them a very valuable lesson:

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:58.130
It’s worth spending more at the outset to
save on human and reconstruction costs.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:00.850
But….

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:05.180
To better illustrate the magnitude of what
we’re talking about, let’s focus on just

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.920
one example.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:12.209
March 11th, 2011 is one of those dates we’ll
all surely remember.

00:03:12.209 --> 00:03:17.771
That day a huge 9.1 magnitude earthquake was
centred in Miyagi Prefecture in Japan, but

00:03:17.771 --> 00:03:21.830
it shook a large part of the country, including
the capital, Tokyo.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:27.290
The Great Eastern Earthquake and the subsequent
tsunami left almost 16,000 dead and more than

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:28.290
6,000 injured.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:34.240
And yes, we’re talking about the same earthquake
that caused the Fukushima disaster.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.060
This was Japan’s latest mega earthquake,
but, folks, the Japanese know that there’s

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:45.500
a 70% chance of another mega earthquake happening
in Tokyo in the next 30 years.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:51.430
And yes, okay, the vast majority, more than
85% of Tokyo buildings are considered resistant,

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:57.819
but the fact that 16,000 hectares of the Japanese
capital is covered in wooden buildings doesn’t

00:03:57.819 --> 00:03:59.489
help.

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:04.290
According to some simulations, the gas leaks
that are commonly produced by a large earthquake

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:09.360
could cause fires throughout the city and
could destroy more than 600 thousand buildings.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.870
So the consequences go far beyond tremors
and possible landslides.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:17.560
And of course, all this generates awareness;
both social and political.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.579
Meanwhile in Chile, they are somewhat behind,
but the social shock of 2010 made them take

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:25.610
risk prevention even more seriously.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:33.080
( An 8.8 magnitude earthquake shakes Chile.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:37.870
The earthquake, lasting one minute, was felt
in the capital, where the electrical service

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:39.029
was interrupted.

00:04:39.029 --> 00:04:41.360
The tsunami that the earthquake caused reached
Hawaii.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:42.360
La Vanguardia 02/27/2019)

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.360
(Chile's earthquake was horrible - but it
could have been so much worse

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.360
Chile is one of South America's richest, best-organised
countries and many of its homes and offices

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:45.360
were built to be earthquake resistant.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:46.360
The Guardian)

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:47.360
Folks, Chile’s earthquake affected 70% of
the country's population, but... thanks to

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:48.680
their anti-seismic regulations, for example,
only two of the capital’s high-rise buildings

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:49.680
collapsed.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.400
And that, in the face of such a strong earthquake,
is quite a success, especially considering

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.150
the precedents of the Latin American region.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:01.180
( “Chile's investments in resilient infrastructure,
early warning systems and urban planning have

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:06.199
managed to ensure that this time the number
of victims is low, despite the earthquake’s

00:05:06.199 --> 00:05:07.199
intensity.”

00:05:07.199 --> 00:05:12.849
Margareta Wahlström, former Office Special
Representative of the United Nations for Disaster

00:05:12.849 --> 00:05:18.490
Risk Reduction.)

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:19.830
And this is what we need to repeat.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.319
As the saying goes: better safe than sorry.

00:05:22.319 --> 00:05:26.199
But, how have Japan and Chile achieved these
levels of risk prevention?

00:05:26.199 --> 00:05:27.610
How do their models differ?

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:34.139
What are the weaknesses and keys to saving
lives?

00:05:34.139 --> 00:05:42.840
(IF THERE’S A RISK, GET AHEAD OF IT)

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:47.930
When we talk about earthquakes, we must keep
two things in mind: they’re unpredictable

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:48.930
and inevitable.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:53.240
However, what can be predicted and avoided
are the risks that arise when an earthquake

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:54.240
occurs.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.030
You see, earthquake damage prevention can
be done in two ways: active or passive.

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:03.210
For example, After the Valdivia earthquake
of 1960, the strongest ever recorded, which

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:08.379
caused a huge tsunami, the Pacific Tsunami
Warning and Mitigation System was created

00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:14.469
by the UNESCO Intergovernmental Oceanographic
Commission in 1965, intending to disseminate

00:06:14.469 --> 00:06:16.849
early tsunami alerts.

00:06:16.849 --> 00:06:22.349
Don't worry, the age of the system shouldn’t
scare you…

00:06:22.349 --> 00:06:23.919
It’s been updated over time.

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:30.240
The fact is that this is one of the best examples
of active prevention.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:38.090
A tsunami in the Pacific has a huge propagation
capacity, and can be very destructive.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:42.279
Usually, there is about a 20 to 30-minute
window between an earthquake happening under

00:06:42.279 --> 00:06:45.110
or near the sea and a tsunami hitting the
nearest coast.

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:50.080
But in the case of the 2011 earthquake in
Japan, in some areas, only two minutes passed

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.960
between the alert and the tsunami making landfall.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:57.870
And of course, in the case of phenomena that
require massive evacuations and which affect

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.300
huge regions, early warnings are extremely
important, massively important.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:03.479
The more time people have, the better.

00:07:03.479 --> 00:07:05.529
Every second counts.

00:07:05.529 --> 00:07:12.319
In fact, a strong earthquake originating in
Chile can cause such a large tsunami that

00:07:12.319 --> 00:07:16.979
it can affect very distant coasts throughout
the Pacific, such as Japan, New Zealand, America

00:07:16.979 --> 00:07:25.009
and even the Philippines.

00:07:25.009 --> 00:07:29.030
And that is precisely why Japan also implemented
its own early warning system, which has been

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:35.530
greatly improved, since the 2011 catastrophe.

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:39.699
Since that year, more than 25 billion dollars
have been invested in earthquake sensors to

00:07:39.699 --> 00:07:41.270
reach 280 points.

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:46.120
It’s the densest and most reliable network
in the world.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:52.020
However, the biggest challenge, by far, is
passive prevention, that is, all those measures

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:55.809
and standards that aim to minimize damage
to buildings, and therefore, victims.

00:07:55.809 --> 00:08:00.759
And in this, Japan is the undisputed leader.

00:08:00.759 --> 00:08:04.569
(Japan’s Strict Building Codes Saved Lives.

00:08:04.569 --> 00:08:05.569
The New York Times)

00:08:05.569 --> 00:08:06.569
But, how exactly does it work?

00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:07.569
Well...

00:08:07.569 --> 00:08:09.589
Check it out.

00:08:09.589 --> 00:08:15.720
Although the first Japanese anti-seismic construction
regulations date back to 1924, today most

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:18.759
of the buildings are built under two very
different regulations.

00:08:18.759 --> 00:08:23.509
The oldest one, called kyu-taishin, dates
back to 1950, and focused on preventing the

00:08:23.509 --> 00:08:24.509
buildings’ collapse.

00:08:24.509 --> 00:08:30.819
The new code or shin-taishin, from 1981, requires
buildings to remain structurally sound and

00:08:30.819 --> 00:08:36.420
show, at most,only cracks after medium-to-strong
earthquakes.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:40.669
These rules have, evidently, been partially
reinforced over the years.

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:44.810
It’s precisely due to this that the structures
of the buildings in Japan are mostly made

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:49.430
out of steel, which is much more flexible
and resistant to movement than rigid concrete.

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:53.730
Furthermore, in some cases, cross beams that
absorb energy from the earthquake are also

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:54.730
installed.

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:59.000
And of course, they also have seismic dampers
at the base of the tallest buildings.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:03.840
Chile, on the other hand, doesn’t require
buildings to withstand damage; regulations

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.370
are more oriented towards preventing their
collapse.

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:10.060
Its current regulation is a bit more similar
to the Japanese one of 1950.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:13.752
In this aspect you can see the huge difference
in vision that Chile has, where the regulation

00:09:13.752 --> 00:09:14.900
is much less strict.

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:19.030
That is to say, in Chile, they seek to save
lives, but without requiring buildings to

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:20.030
remain unscathed.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:27.830
And there’s a fundamental reason for this:
the cost.

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:32.370
Building a building as resilient as those
in Japan can cost around 10 to 15% more than

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:33.370
a regular one.

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:38.010
And of course, this makes housing prices in
newer and more resistant buildings more expensive.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:41.500
The philosophy is: “If you live here, you’ll
be safe.”

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:43.930
But, just a second.

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:48.660
We’re assuming that rules are always met
and codes are always followed, that everyone

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:52.030
is working for the common good, when the truth
may be quite different.

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:56.580
In fact, do you know what the number one problem
is that affects security and can cost many

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:57.580
lives?

00:09:57.580 --> 00:09:58.580
Think about it for a second...

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:03.510
Precisely, you got it.

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:09.450
(ATTENTION, CORRUPTION KILLS)

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:11.800
Yes, you heard correctly.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:16.030
It may surprise you, but corruption is, without
a doubt, the greatest threat to security in

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:19.740
terms of earthquakes.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:23.710
And no, neither Japan nor Chile are free of
this scourge because, even though Japan has

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:28.421
the image of an ideal country and Chile ranks
a respectable number 27, in the corruption

00:10:28.421 --> 00:10:32.630
perception index, from time to time, scandals
like this one crop up.

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:37.370
The KYB Corporation, a Japanese company dedicated
to the development and manufacture of seismic

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:42.000
dampers, admitted at the end of 2018, that
they had falsified the quality and performance

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.640
data of their products for more than a decade.

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:49.160
To this date, they haven’t yet been able
to quantify the real scope of the risk that

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:52.830
this entails, but it’s estimated that the
effectiveness of the falsified products could

00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:58.660
be at around 86% of the minimum requirements
required by Law.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:02.330
Buildings as important as Tokyo City Hall,
the Olympic Aquatics Center and the Ariake

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:08.740
Arena, home of the 2020 Olympic Games, used
seismic dampers from this company.

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:14.980
And as if that weren’t enough, the tallest
tower in the world does too.

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:20.180
Tokyo Skytree among buildings linked to data
scandal at earthquake shock absorber-maker

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:21.180
KYB Corp..

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:22.280
The Japan Times)

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:26.070
Although this case is quite serious, the truth
is that Japan doesn’t have much corruption,

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:30.360
and that means that seismic regulations are
followed most of the time.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:36.090
Chile, on the other hand, has the lowest corruption
level in all of Latin America, which has contributed

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:40.340
to the fact that anti-seismic regulations
in construction are complied with in the vast

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:41.340
majority of cases.

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:45.650
Although, as we’ve seen, they are far less
stringent than Japan’s.

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:50.920
“We have a law that states that designs
of a certain height have to be reviewed by

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:54.940
an independent team that verifies that the
standards are being met.

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:56.730
No one builds without this backing.”

00:11:56.730 --> 00:12:01.610
Sergio Sepúlveda, geology scholar from the
University of Chile, to El País.)

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:02.610
But you know what?

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:06.650
It’s precisely this broad compliance with
regulations that makes Chile a much safer

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:13.720
country to live in than, for example, Mexico,
during an earthquake.

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:18.920
Corruption and negligence caused landslides
during the Mexico City earthquake.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:20.120
EFE)

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:25.110
Mexico, one of the countries with the highest
corruption rates in the region, is also a

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:27.350
highly seismic country.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:32.110
The difference with Chile is that in Mexico’s
cities, every time there’s a strong earthquake

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:35.110
several buildings collapse, leaving numerous
victims.

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:38.770
And although not all earthquakes have the
same characteristics, for reasons such as

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:45.190
the type of soil and depth, Mexico City lost
228 people in the city alone, and 369 across

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:49.840
the entire country in the earthquake on September
19 2017.

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:54.270
On the other hand, the 2015 earthquake in
Coquimbo, Chile, which was felt with an intensity

00:12:54.270 --> 00:13:01.170
of 7 in Santiago, only caused 15 fatalities
throughout the country.

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:03.210
A great example of earthquake preparation.

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:07.080
Therefore, folks, Mexico and other highly
seismic countries need to catch up and work

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:10.510
hard and honestly for the safety of their
citizens.

